# identity.config — what is true about the PERSON, on this install, forever.
#
# THE SPLIT THIS FILE EXISTS TO MAKE. The engine's `identity.config` holds COMPANY_NAME and
# CEO_NAME in one file. Under a three-layer model those two belong to different layers with
# different lifetimes, and keeping them together is how a single-company assumption survives a
# multi-company design:
#
#   CEO_NAME      install-invariant  ->  HERE. One CEO, one machine; it can go in a system prompt.
#   COMPANY_NAME  varies per thread  ->  companies/<id>/company.md. It can NEVER go in a system
#                                        prompt: one chat lease serves every company and the
#                                        thread switches underneath it, so a company name fixed
#                                        at spawn would be a lie the moment he switched.
#
# So there is no COMPANY_NAME in this file, and its absence is the design rather than an omission.
#
# FORMAT: plain `NAME="value"` assignments.
# NEVER put a credential here.

# --- REQUIRED --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Blank on purpose. THIS IS NOT FILLED IN BY WHOEVER BUILT THE FOLDER.
#
# The design's §2.2 is explicit that the app asks and never invents, and `entity.rs` rules that an
# empty registry "resolves nothing, refuses every root, and the app's job in that state is to ask
# ... never to invent an answer". The same rule governs a name. Guessing how a person wants to be
# addressed from a commit author line is exactly the invention that rule forbids.
#
# Measured on 2026-09-06: `~/Library/Application Support/com.richos.app/config.json` has
# `"user_name": null`. Nothing on this machine has been told the answer yet. The first-run
# interview is what fills this in.
CEO_NAME=""                # how Rich addresses and refers to him

# --- Optional context ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Left blank, each is simply omitted rather than rendered as a TODO. An honest gap beats a
# placeholder that reads like a value.
CEO_TIMEZONE=""            # e.g. "America/New_York"
CEO_NOTES=""               # anything true about him on turn one, regardless of company
